* T-4, Turchin 1988 p. 538: the compilation strategy is a CHOICE, and the
* search is what makes the choice from measurement rather than from a rule.
*
* The two ends of the compilation-interpretation axis produce different
* programs here, and the search keeps the smaller. `Accum` below walks an
* unknown expression into an accumulator that grows by one term per step, so no
* configuration of `Accum` recurs exactly and the compilative whistle fires
* late: its residue is the larger one. The interpretive end loops back on the
* first-order neighborhood instead (Turchin 1988 section 4), which terminates
* for his reason rather than by embedding -- there are finitely many
* first-order neighborhoods -- and emits the smaller residue.
*
* `refal residualize-driven` therefore searches: it drives both ends, measures
* each residue the same way, and keeps the smaller. The report says which end
* won and what the other one cost, so the choice is observable rather than
* asserted. `metasystem-unroll.ref` is the other direction: there the
* compilative end reaches zero residual work and the interpretive end is not
* even run.
*
* This file used to record something stronger -- that the compilative end
* produced NO program at all, failing with `ground driver does not support
* unbound residual variables`. That failure was the ground matcher's
* nested-bracket defect rather than a property of the axis; with the defect
* fixed both ends produce a residue, and the search's choice is a measurement
* rather than a rescue.
$ENTRY Go {
  e.X = <Accum () e.X>;
}

* Move every term of the argument into the accumulator, one at a time. The
* accumulator is an argument rather than a result, so each call's argument is
* larger than the one before it and the sequence of configurations never
* repeats -- which is exactly the shape the compilative whistle cannot catch.
Accum {
  (e.Acc) = e.Acc;
  (e.Acc) t.C e.Rest = <Accum (e.Acc t.C) e.Rest>;
}
